<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="editorial" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Struct Heart</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Struct Heart</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Structural Heart</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2474-8706</issn><issn pub-type="epub">2474-8714</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">40894367</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12399226</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S2474-8706(25)00295-7</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.shj.2025.100703</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">100703</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Editorial</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Intravascular Lithotripsy in Mitral Annulus Calcification-Related Mitral Stenosis: Hope or Hype?</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au1"><name><surname>Urena</surname><given-names>Marina</given-names></name><degrees>MD, PhD</degrees><email>marina.urena-alcazar@aphp.fr</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">c</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp">&#x02217;</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>a</label>Cardiology Department, Bichat Claude Bernard Hospital, Paris, France</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>b</label>Paris City University, Paris, France</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>c</label>INSERM, Paris, France</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><label>&#x02217;</label>Address correspondence to: Marina Urena Bichat Hospital, Paris City University 46 rue Henri Huchard, Paris 75018, France. <email>marina.urena-alcazar@aphp.fr</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>17</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>17</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>9</volume><issue>8</issue><elocation-id>100703</elocation-id><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 Published by Elsevier Inc. on behalf of Cardiovascular Research Foundation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><copyright-holder/><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><related-article related-article-type="commentary-article" id="d36e9" ext-link-type="doi" xlink:href="10.1016/j.shj.2025.100683"/></article-meta></front><body><p id="p0010">Although still less common than rheumatic mitral stenosis (MS) globally, the incidence of degenerative MS is growing in high-income countries primarily due to population aging.<xref rid="bib1" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> The principal underlying mechanism of degenerative MS is mitral annulus calcification (MAC).<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> While MAC more commonly leads to mitral regurgitation, it can also cause stenosis. Unlike rheumatic MS&#x02014;which is characterized by commissural fusion&#x02014;MAC-related MS results from a reduction in the effective mitral orifice area, impaired diastolic expansion of the annulus, and restricted anterior leaflet motion due to calcific extension into the leaflets.<xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr"><sup>3</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref></p><p id="p0015">MAC shares pathophysiological mechanisms with atherosclerosis and is associated with conditions that increase mechanical stress on the mitral annulus. It is particularly prevalent in patients with calcific aortic stenosis, left ventricular hypertrophy, and systemic hypertension.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> Patients with MAC are typically older, more often women, and commonly present with multiple cardiovascular risk factors and severe comorbidities, including chronic kidney disease.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> Importantly, MAC has been independently associated with increased mortality, and MAC-related MS carries a significant burden of morbidity and mortality.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref></p><p id="p0020">Despite growing recognition, MAC-related MS remains a clinical challenge and an unmet therapeutic need. First, diagnosis is often more complex than in rheumatic mitral valve disease. Patients with MAC frequently present with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, and the resulting elevation in left atrial pressure can lead to increased transmitral gradients, even in the absence of true anatomical MS.<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> Second, treatment options for MAC-related MS are limited and technically demanding. Surgical intervention is particularly challenging due to the difficulty of securing sutures in heavily calcified tissue. Various surgical techniques have been developed to address this issue, but they may be associated with increased perioperative risk and complications. Transcatheter mitral valve replacement (TMVR), often using valves originally designed for the aortic position, presents additional challenges. These include the large dimensions of the mitral annulus, often exceeding the size range of currently available devices, the absence of circumferential calcification needed for securing anchoring, and a particularly high risk of left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, due to the frequent presence of left ventricular hypertrophy and small left ventricule cavities. Although several dedicated TMVR devices have been used in patients with MAC-related MS, clinical experience remains limited, and the rate of screening failure for TMVR in this population is notably high.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref> Lastly, even when early procedural outcomes are favorable, 1-year mortality remains high, underscoring the challenges of appropriate patient selection for this therapy.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref></p><p id="p0025">In this issue of the journal, Giustino and colleagues report the results of a novel percutaneous therapy for patients with MAC-related MS: the MATRIX technique or intravascular lithotripsy of the mitral annulus.<xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> A total of seven patients with MAC-related MS, all considered very high risk for both percutaneous and surgical therapies, underwent valvuloplasty of the mitral annulus using shockwave balloons. Nearly 60% of patients experienced a reduction of at least 50% in mean transmitral gradient. Among the six patients with available echocardiographic follow-up data, 50% (3 patients) achieved a 30-day mean gradient of less than 5 mmHg, 33% had gradients below 8 mmHg, and only one patient had a mean gradient of 13 mmHg.<xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref></p><p id="p0030">Although the efficacy of this new therapy appears inferior to that of TMVR or surgery, it offers several advantages, including a short duration&#x02014;approximately 30 minutes&#x02014;and an excellent safety profile, with no patients developing significant mitral regurgitation or experiencing complications following the intervention.</p><p id="p0035">Unlike rheumatic MS, percutaneous balloon mitral commissurotomy is not indicated in MAC-related MS because the absence of commissural fusion renders this therapy ineffective.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref> However, intravascular lithotripsy may induce multiple microfragmentation of calcifications reducing stiffness and potentially improving anterior leaflet mobility and diastolic mitral annular expansion.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref></p><p id="p0040">We congratulate the authors on the initial results of this modified intravascular lithotripsy technique, which appears highly promising. While previous case series have reported the use of shockwave therapy,<xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> it was typically combined with mitral balloon valvuloplasty using large semicompliant balloons, which carry a potential risk of mitral regurgitation. In contrast, the use of three shockwave balloons in this series, with final balloon sizing oversized by 20% relative to the mitral annular dimensions, enabled an effective reduction in the transmitral gradient while maintaining a low risk of complications.</p><p id="p0045">Although encouraging, these results are highly preliminary and require confirmation in larger studies. Several concerns remain. First, the anatomical characteristics of patients who responded to the therapy were not reported. Additional data on the severity and extent of valvular calcification based on computed tomography imaging would be valuable. Second, the clinical benefit of the therapy was not clearly demonstrated. Moreover, the durability of the hemodynamic improvement as a standalone therapy may be limited over time (e.g., 6 months to 1 year), potentially resembling the transient effects of balloon valvuloplasty in aortic stenosis. As such, repeated interventions might be necessary.<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> However, despite its favorable safety profile, the therapy remains invasive, which could limit the feasibility of repeated interventions. In this regard, the development of noninvasive approaches&#x02014;such as pulsed cavitational ultrasound, which is currently under investigation for the treatment of aortic stenosis&#x02014;may represent a more promising alternative. Additionally, although no complications were reported in this study, embolic protection devices were used in nearly 60% of patients, indicating that the true risk of embolic events remains uncertain. While cavitational ultrasound has been suggested to carry a low embolic risk, this has yet to be definitively established. Finally, head-to-head comparisons are needed to determine the optimal strategy&#x02014;whether shockwave balloon therapy alone or in combination with semicompliant valvuloplasty balloons offers superior safety and efficacy.</p><p id="p0050">In conclusion, MAC-related MS remains a significant clinical challenge. Intravascular lithotripsy using the MATRIX technique appears to be a promising therapeutic option for these patients with acceptable efficacy and a very low complication rate. Although durability of results might be limited, this approach has the advantage of overcoming screening failure issues encountered with other therapies and may help identify patients most likely to benefit from intervention, thereby reducing the risk of futile treatment. However, these results are very preliminary, and largest studies are necessary to demonstrate the safety and efficacy of this novel therapy.</p><sec id="sec1a"><title>Funding</title><p id="p0060">The authors have no funding to report.</p></sec><sec id="sec1"><title>Disclosure Statement</title><p id="p0055">M. Urena received consultant fee/honoraria/speaker's bureau from Edwards, Medtronic, and Abbott.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebib0010"><title>References</title><ref id="bib1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref1"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Coffey</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Roberts-Thomson</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>